\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a novel multi-stage method for colour image segmentation, with a primary focus on vessels segmentation in retinal fundus images, using a U-Net based architecture. Our approach tackles challenges posed by varying image resolutions through a coarse-to-fine segmentation pipeline. It begins with a rough segmentation at varying scales, guided by a traditional CNN and progressively refines results to find a target resolution. It culminates with detailed segmentation at a target scale with a smaller window sliding step, compared to previous stages. We train and validate our method using four publicly available datasets FIVES, DRHAGIS, HRF, and STARE - and demonstrate superior performance compared to traditional sliding window techniques. Notably, our model achieves high accuracy with relatively few training images. The entire framework is open-sourced and adaptable to a wide range of image segmentation tasks.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{U-Net, segmentation, deep learning, vessel segmentation, medical imaging, fundus images}

\section{Introduction}

The process of image segmentation has been for many years a challenge, that was only partially solveable. Many scientists have developed specific algorithms which targeted a range of problems or a concrete problem, for which a solution was proposed with an aim of solving the issue of extracting an object or objects of interest from the image. Many of such approaches could be used in other domains. Other approaches were almost too specific with an incorporation of domain knowledge into the algorithm itself, which have often made them difficult to transfer into other applications.

This changed with an advent of deep learning and introduction of convoutional neural networks. In the year 2012 Krizhevsky et al \cite{krizhevsky2012imagenet} introduced ImageNet, which achieved a new level of image recognition accuracy. It won the ImageNet competition that year, and inspired a plethora of other researchers to improve upon it. This milestone in image classification allowed a development of new class of segmentation algorithms.

One of the challenges in research was to segment biomedical images. It was partially solved by Ronneberger et al. \cite{ronneberger2015unet} by introduction of \textit{U-Net} architecture. The \textit{U-Net} is designed to work with very few training images and to yield more precise segmentations. It consists of a contracting path to capture context and a symmetric expanding path that enables precise localization. It proved to be major step forward in image segmentation, by providing a generic framework that can work with images under broad set of conditions, whilst maintaing a high quality of the output. However it does come with a number of drawbacks amongst which are: sensitivity to input image resolution and sensitivity to segmentation window location, and high demand for GPU memory which is limiting the segmentation window size (\textit{WS}). This can result in major differences of segmentation outputs.

In this article, we address key challenges in image segmentation by proposing a multi-step framework for training a \textit{U-Net} network. Our approach is desgiend to be adaptable to images with resolutions different from those used during training. To maximize the quality of the segmented output, we evaluate the segmentation results at multiple image scales and select the most effective one. We also explore, how the quality of segmentation changes, when the \textit{U-Net} model is applied at different scales. This analysis helps us to understand, how the network's performance is affected by the resolution of the image during inference, even when model was trained at a fixed resolution. Additionally, we aim to determine the level of annotation effort needed, to begin achieving satisfactory results. Our experiments are conducted on four publicly available vessel segmentation datasets, each accompanied by manual segmentation masks. This allows us to mix training and validation sets across different resolutions and evaluate, how well a \textit{U-Net} trained on one dataset generalizes to others.

The article is divided into seven chapters. The first chapter serves as an introduction. The second provides a brief review of relevant and up-to-date articles published in recent years on \textit{U-Net} segmentation, with a focus on medical imaging. The third chapter presents the theoretical foundation of our approach, including illustrations and diagrams that outline key components of the algorithm. The fourth chapter details the experiments conducted and their corresponding results. The fifth chapter summarizes the key findings observed during experimentation. Chapter six offers a conclusion and outlines directions for future work. Finally, chapter seven includes a declaration of no competing interests.

The main contributions of this work are:
\begin{itemize} 
  \item A general and modular framework for medical image segmentation using \textit{U-Net}, designed to handle varying image resolutions and object scales. 
  \item A multi-scale rough segmentation step that processes images at multiple predefined scales using a sliding window approach. 
  \item A \textit{CNN}-based scoring mechanism that selects the optimal segmentation scale for each image based on predicted segmentation quality. 
  \item A probability heatmap fusion strategy that averages overlapping predictions from multiple windows to enhance consistency and accuracy. 
  \item Experimental validation of cross-dataset generalization, demonstrating that models trained on one dataset (e.g., \textit{FIVES}) perform well on others (e.g., \textit{DRHAGIS}, \textit{HRF}, \textit{STARE}). 
  \item An analysis of how the number of training images affects segmentation quality, providing insight into the annotation effort required for satisfactory performance. 
  \item Full support for configurable parameters, including window size, step size, and scale percentages, allowing seamless adaptation to new datasets. 
  \item An open-source implementation, available on GitHub under the name Hybrid \textit{U-Net} Segmentation (\textit{HUS}) \cite{palles77unetsegmentation}, to support reproducibility and future research. 
\end{itemize}

\section{Related works}

The field of image segmentation - particularly medical image segmentation and the application of \textit{U-Net} - has seen extensive research in recent years. Given the breadth of work in this area, it is challenging to cover it in its entirety. Therefore, we chose to focus on the most recent advances, highlighting how other researchers have approached and addressed the problem.

Ren et al. \cite{ren2022improved} proposed an improved \textit{U-Net}-based method for retinal vessel image segmentation, enhancing the original architecture to achieve greater accuracy and robustness. Their improvements include modifications to both the network structure and the training process. Liu et al. \cite{liu2023retina} introduced a three-path \textit{U-Net} model for retina image segmentation, which leverages multiple pathways to capture features at different levels, resulting in improved segmentation performance. Das et al. \cite{das2024assessment} evaluated the performance of the standard \textit{U-Net} for retinal blood vessel segmentation, confirming its effectiveness for this task. Yun et al. \cite{yun2020multi} proposed a Multi-Path Recurrent \textit{U-Net} for segmenting retinal fundus images, incorporating both multiple paths and recurrent units to enhance feature extraction. Similarly, Huang et al. \cite{huang2023retinal} developed an improved \textit{U-Net} architecture based on residual modules, achieving increased robustness and better segmentation results in retinal vascular images.

Sevastopolsky \cite{sevastopolsky2017optic} modified the \textit{U-Net} convolutional neural network for optic disc (\textit{ED}) and eye cup (\textit{EC}) segmentation, contributing to automated glaucoma detection. Chen et al. \cite{chen2024optic} introduced an \textit{Attention U-Net} incorporating a residual mechanism for (\textit{EC}, \textit{ED}) segmentation, which enhanced the accuracy of glaucoma detection. Chen and Lv \cite{Chen2024} developed a segmentation model tailored for (\textit{EC}, \textit{ED}) in fundus images, emphasizing its relevance in early-stage glaucoma diagnosis. Chowdhury et al. \cite{Chowdhury2022} proposed a \textit{U-Net}-based method leveraging entropy sampling, achieving promising results on the Drishti-GS and RIM-ONE V3 datasets. Sudhan et al. \cite{Sudhan2022} designed a deep learning framework incorporating \textit{U-Net} for both segmentation and classification of glaucoma, demonstrating strong performance on the ORIGA dataset. Tadisetty et al. \cite{Tadisetty2023} focused on identifying the boundaries of the (\textit{EC}, \textit{ED}) regions in glaucoma patients through segmentation, thereby improving diagnostic accuracy.

Siddique et al. \cite{Siddique2021} reviewed \textit{U-Net} and its variants for medical image segmentation, discussing their theory and applications. Suri et al. \cite{Suri2022} provided a detailed analysis of the \textit{U-Net} architecture for vascular and non-vascular image segmentation, incorporating pruning and explainable AI. Kotaridis et al. \cite{Kotaridis2022} applied a \textit{U-Net} architecture for semantic segmentation on Sentinel-2 data, demonstrating its effectiveness in remote sensing. Azad et al. \cite{Azad2024} reviewed the success of \textit{U-Net} in medical image segmentation, highlighting its impact and advancements. Baheti et al. \cite{Baheti2020} introduced \textit{Eff-UNet}, a novel architecture for semantic segmentation in unstructured environments, showing improved performance.

Wang et al. \cite{wang2019recurrent} proposed a \textit{Recurrent U-Net} for resource-constrained segmentation, enhancing efficiency and accuracy. Loew \cite{Loew2021} presented \textit{DC-UNet}, a dual-channel efficient \textit{CNN} for medical image segmentation, rethinking the \textit{U-Net} architecture. Yousefi et al \cite{Yousefi2023} developed a new hybrid segmentation algorithm, \textit{UNet-GOA}, demonstrating its effectiveness in medical image segmentation. Akyel et al. \cite{akyel2023unet} introduced \textit{U-Net-RCB7}, an image segmentation algorithm for skin cancer detection, showing promising results. Li \cite{li2024improving} improved polygon image segmentation by enhancing the \textit{U-Net} architecture, achieving better segmentation accuracy.

Many existing studies aim to enhance the performance of \textit{U-Net} across a range of applications. A common theme among these efforts is the focus on segmenting disconnected or localized objects, often overlooking use cases like vessel segmentation, where structures are continuous and span the entire image. Additionally, relatively few works address the challenge of handling images with varying resolutions - a critical factor in real-world medical imaging scenarios. Notably, many proposed improvements to \textit{U-Net} result in only marginal performance gains, typically in the range of 1–2% absolute accuracy, as the original \textit{U-Net} already performs strongly, often achieving over 95% accuracy under favorable conditions. In such cases, the increased model complexity introduced by these modifications may not be justified by the limited benefits. In contrast, some performance gains can be achieved through more lightweight and efficient enhancements, without the need for substantial architectural changes.

\section{Methodology}

The idea behind our approach originated from our work on segmenting fundus eye images (\textit{FEI}). In recent years, numerous high-quality datasets have been published for this purpose. From these, we selected four publicly available datasets for our experiments: \textit{FIVES} \cite{jin2022fives}, consisting of 1,200 images with a resolution of 2048$\times$2048 pixels; \textit{DRHAGIS} \cite{holm2017drhagis}, containing 80 images at 4752$\times$3168 pixels; \textit{HRF} \cite{budai2013robust}, which includes 45 images at 3504$\times$2336 pixels; and \textit{STARE} \cite{hoover2000locating}, from which we used 20 images at 700$\times$605 pixels resolution.

An overview of our segmentation framework is presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:general_approach}, and the subsequent sections provide a detailed explanation of each component.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[height=300px]{figures/image1generalapproach.pdf}
  \caption{Proposed workflow steps}
  \label{fig:general_approach}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Data clean up}
\label{subsec:step1}

This step is a crucial part of the data preparation process. To ensure consistency and avoid disruptions, we perform data cleanup by removing any temporary processing files that may have remained from previous runs. This helps guarantee that results are not influenced by residual artifacts and are fully repeatable. Additionally, this cleanup process enables the automation of experiments, thereby minimizing the need for manual intervention.

\subsection{Training data preparation}
\label{subsec:step2}

In preparing data for \textit{U-Net} training, it is essential to extract square windows (ideally with dimensions of $2^n$) from the input images. These windows are then fed into the training pipeline to iteratively refine the model's performance. Although \textit{U-Net} is known for its efficiency and accuracy under favorable conditions, a significant challenge lies in determining which parts of the image should be selected for training.To address this challenge, several key questions must be considered:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Which locations should be chosen to ensure that windows cover both object and background regions effectively?
  \item How many windows should be sampled from each image?
  \item How many of these windows should predominantly contain the object of interest?
  \item How many windows should cover the background?
  \item What is the optimal ratio between object-containing and background-only windows?
  \item What should be the size of each window?
  \item Which window augmentation techniques should be employed, particularly in data-scarce scenarios?
\end{itemize}

Below, we present our approach to answering the aforementioned questions by constructing a mechanism for automatic data generation. We use the \textit{FIVES} dataset \cite{jin2022fives} as the primary input for training the \textit{U-Net} model. The original dataset, consisting of 1,200 images, is divided into two subsets. The first subset, referred to as the \textit{U-Net} Main Training Set (\textit{UNETMTS}), includes 900 images and is used for model training. The second subset, called the \textit{U-Net} Segmentation Validation Set (\textit{UNETSVS}), comprises the remaining 300 images and is used to validate segmentation performance.

\subsubsection{Mask finding}
\label{subsubsection:maskfinding}

Each image from both subsets is processed using a mask generation algorithm, which consists of the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] The image is converted from RGB to grayscale.  
  \item[(b)] A threshold for binarization is computed using the following heuristic formula:
  \[
  \text{threshold} = \frac{\text{mean}(\text{grayscale\_image})}{3} - 5
  \]
  This threshold is used to generate a binary mask, referred to as \textit{mask}.  
  \item[(c)] The binary image is then processed using a flood fill algorithm starting from all four corners, producing an additional mask, denoted as \textit{mask\_new}.  
  \item[(d)] The original \textit{mask} is combined with \textit{mask\_new} using a pixel-wise summation, updating the original mask values.  
  \item[(e)] Finally, the updated \textit{mask} is subjected to morphological operations: erosion followed by dilation, using a 20$\times$20 pixel kernel.
\end{enumerate}

The generated mask is applied to the original image using a logical AND operation. Specifically, any pixel located within the outer layer of the mask is set to black, while all other pixels retain their original values. The result of this masking procedure is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:image_masking}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train_mask.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train_masked.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Input image & \scriptsize (b) Mask & \scriptsize (c) Image with \\
    \scriptsize \scriptsize & \scriptsize & \scriptsize mask applied
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Image Masking Results}
  \label{fig:image_masking}
\end{figure}

After the mask is generated, the image is trimmed from the left, right, top, and bottom edges to ensure that no completely black vertical or horizontal lines remain along the borders of the trimmed image.

\subsubsection{Random windows}
\label{subsubsec:randomwindows}

The initial set, \textit{UNETMTS}, is reduced to a specified value referred to as \textit{TRDTSZ} (training data size), which is initially set to 900. However, this value is varied and analyzed in later sections of this article (see Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) to investigate whether the full set of 900 images is necessary, or if smaller subsets can still yield high segmentation accuracy. The goal is to explore how data-hungry the \textit{U-Net} model is when aiming to achieve optimal results. Once the value of \textit{TRDTSZ} is selected, \textit{UNETMTS} is randomly divided into two subsets: a training set (\textit{DPSTR}) and a test set (\textit{DPSTS}), using a 75\% to 25\% split.

From each image, a set of training windows is randomly sampled. Two types of windows are defined: object-type windows (\textit{OBTW}) and background-type windows (\textit{BKTW}). A window is classified as \textit{OBTW} if the number of pixels corresponding to the ground truth label exceeds a minimum threshold (e.g., 15\%). Conversely, a window is labeled as \textit{BKTW} if the proportion of object pixels does not exceed a maximum threshold (e.g., 5\%). We also define the target ratio of object to background windows per image (e.g., 50\%) to ensure a representative sampling that reflects the typical pixel distribution between object and background regions in medical images.

The underlying motivation for this windowing strategy is to balance object and background representation during training. This balance is reinforced by employing a weighted binary cross-entropy loss function in the \textit{U-Net} implementation, which helps address class imbalance.

The size of each sampled window (\textit{WS}) is typically set to either 64$\times$64 or 128$\times$128 pixels. Input images are rescaled after mask generation to either 512$\times$512 or 1024$\times$1024 pixels. Larger values of \textit{WS} and image resolution significantly increase GPU memory consumption - an important consideration given that our hardware setup was a GPU with only 8GB of memory.

The percentage values used in this step are based on intuition and can be adjusted depending on the type of images, particularly when the ratio of object pixels to background pixels varies. For instance, these values would differ when segmenting structures such as the eye cup (\textit{EC}) or optic disc (\textit{ED}) in other \textit{FEI} datasets. The goal of this strategy is to capture a wide range of spatial arrangements of the target object within randomly selected windows, thereby enriching the training dataset for the \textit{U-Net} model used in later stages of the system.

The final component of this step involves applying data augmentation techniques. These augmentations increase the number of training samples and enhance the model’s generalization ability.

\subsection{U-Net network training}
\label{subsec:step3)}

The windows extracted in Section~\ref{subsec:step2} are grouped into training batches, with a predefined number of epochs and a selected (\textit{WS}) that matches the input dimensions of the \textit{U-Net} model. The input windows, referred to as \textit{X} windows, consist of three color channels - red, green, and blue - with pixel values normalized to the range [0, 1]. The corresponding output windows, denoted as \textit{Y} windows, represent the segmentation masks and are converted into binary images with two channels: channel 0 (index 0) represents the background, and channel 1 (index 1) represents the target object.

For each pixel in the ground truth segmentation mask, if the pixel belongs to the background, channel 0 is set to 1 and channel 1 to 0. Conversely, if the pixel belongs to the object (in this case, a vessel), channel 0 is set to 0 and channel 1 to 1.

\subsection{U-Net preliminary multi resolution segmentation}
\label{subsec:step4}

A challenge we previously encountered is that a direct, pixel-to-pixel segmentation using a standard \textit{U-Net} tends to be vulnerable to several issues:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Resolution sensitivity:} A key limitation of standard \textit{U-Net} models is their limited ability to handle varying image resolutions. For example, if the training, validation, and test datasets consist of images captured under consistent conditions, such as similar lighting, noise levels, sharpness, and resolution, the trained \textit{U-Net} typically performs well. However, when validation or test images differ significantly in scale (e.g., being much larger or smaller than the training samples), the model often struggles to generate accurate segmentations. This limitation arises from the nature of convolutional layers, which learn to recognize features at the specific scales encountered during training. When tested on images outside that range, performance often degrades. As a result, achieving robust segmentation across images of varying resolutions requires determining an optimal target scale for effective segmentation.
  \item \textbf{Need for randomized window sampling:} The \textit{U-Net} architecture relies on a randomized window selection strategy, due to the unpredictable positioning of target objects within images. In vessel segmentation tasks, and indeed in many image segmentation problems, structures of interest may appear in various locations and orientations. Moreover, vessels may be seen at different angles and rotations. Thus, to ensure the model learns robust, rotation-invariant features, the training process must incorporate windows sampled from diverse regions and orientations within the input images.
\end{enumerate}

These two major challenges underscore the limitations of \textit{U-Net} segmentation, particularly in high-precision applications such as medical imaging. To address these issues, we propose a targeted approach specifically designed to overcome them. Our method begins with a rough segmentation step, in which the input image is segmented at multiple scales using a sliding window technique. This process produces a set of candidate segmentations across different resolutions. This stage is referred to as the rough segmentation step (\textit{RSS}). 

The primary goal of the \textit{RSS} is to quickly produce preliminary segmentation results in order to identify a semi-optimal scale. This information is later used to guide the search for the most suitable resolution at which the \textit{U-Net} model is likely to perform optimally.

The resulting binary (black-and-white) segmented images are then passed to the next stage (described in Section~\ref{subsec:step5}), where a dedicated \textit{CNN} model is trained to evaluate and select the most accurate segmentation result.

In terms of image preprocessing, we support optional contrast enhancement techniques. However, in the majority of our experiments, these enhancements were disabled, as they did not yield noticeable improvements in segmentation quality.

\subsubsection{Windows mesh creation}
\label{subssubsec:windows_mesh_creation}

Once the image has been prepared for segmentation - by generating a mask as described in Section~\ref{subsubsection:maskfinding} - it typically contains a black border surrounding the scene, with the retina centered. This preprocessed image is then analyzed across three color channels: red, green, and blue. These are the same input channels used by the \textit{U-Net} model during training.

Each color channel is divided into a collection of meshes (i.e., image patches) by sliding a window across the image. Mesh extraction begins with an offset of 0 and proceeds up to $\textit{WS}-1$, using a predefined sliding step denoted as \textit{SS}. While it is not required for $\textit{WS} \bmod \textit{SS} = 0$, it is essential that $\textit{SS} < \textit{WS}$ to ensure overlapping patches and continuous coverage of the entire image.

The idea of meshes can be shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:meshes}
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_0.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_1.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Mesh with offset 0 & \scriptsize (b) Mesh with offset 32 \\
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_2.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_3.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Mesh with offset 64 & \scriptsize (b) Mesh with offset 96 \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Four meshes generated for an image of size 1024$\times$1024 pixels, \textit{WS} of 128$\times$128 pixels, and \textit{WSS} set to 32}
  \label{fig:meshes}
\end{figure}

Each mesh allows the input image to be segmented using a different window configuration. This approach is crucial for capturing diverse spatial variations within the image, as each mesh is processed independently by the trained \textit{U-Net} model. For every mesh, the \textit{U-Net} produces a probability map, where each pixel is assigned a probability value indicating the likelihood of it belonging to the object or the background.

Due to overlapping meshes, a single pixel may appear in multiple segmentations, resulting in multiple probability estimates. These values are averaged to generate a final probability map, or heatmap, which represents the overall likelihood of each pixel being part of the object. In this heatmap, brighter pixels correspond to higher confidence in object presence.

Finally, the heatmap is binarized using a fixed threshold of 127, producing a black-and-white segmentation mask. An example of this process is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:meshes}, where four meshes contribute to the heatmap visualized in Fig.~\ref{fig:heatmap1}. This method is referred to as the Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}).

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[height=0.15\textheight]{figures/presegment_rough_original.png} &
    \includegraphics[height=0.15\textheight]{figures/presegment_rough_heatmap.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Input image & \scriptsize (b) Pre binarization heatmap \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Input image from \cite{jin2022fives} along with generated heatmap}
  \label{fig:heatmap1}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Data preparation for \textit{CNN} training}

As described in Section~\ref{subsec:step2}, the main dataset \cite{jin2022fives} was partitioned to include a subset named \textit{UNETSVS}, which is used for training the \textit{CNN} in the subsequent evaluation stage. All images in this subset are processed through the (\textit{RSS}).

We perform segmentation at multiple scales, ranging from 20\% to 100\% of the original image size, in increments of 10\%. This produces a series of black-and-white segmentation outputs for each image at varying resolutions. Figure~\ref{fig:roughsegmentations} presents the segmentation results for five selected scales: 20\%, 40\%, 60\%, 80\%, and 100\%. It is apparent from the visual comparison that the quality of the segmentations varies significantly across different scales.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_20.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_40.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Scale of 20\% & \scriptsize (b) Scale of 40\% \\  
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_60.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_80.png} \\
    \scriptsize (c) Scale of 80\% & \scriptsize (d) Scale of 100\% \\  
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_100.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_truth.png} \\
    \scriptsize (e) Scale of 100\% & \scriptsize (f) Groud truth \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Different scales of input image for \textit{CNN} segmentation assessment training}
  \label{fig:roughsegmentations}
\end{figure}

To objectively assess which of the segmentation results is most optimal, we design and train a simple \textit{CNN}-based scoring network. This network consists of a single output neuron that produces a score between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates the poorest segmentation quality and 1 indicates the best (see Fig.~\ref{fig:cnn_network}). The trained \textit{CNN} is employed in the final segmentation stage, as described in Section~\ref{subsec:step6}.

\subsection{CNN training for segmentation results assessment}
\label{subsec:step5}

Using the \textit{UNETSVS} set, we segment each image into nine black-and-white binarizations, producing a total of $300 \times (9 + 1) = 3000$ samples for training the \textit{CNN}. The additional term in the formula accounts for the ground truth image (\textit{GT}), which serves as the expected output from the segmentation algorithm.

This ground truth image is also used to compute the Jaccard index \cite{jaccard1901}, which measures the similarity between the segmented output and its corresponding \textit{GT}. The index yields a value between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates a completely incorrect segmentation and 1 indicates a perfect match.

The \textit{CNN} architecture used for evaluation is implemented directly in the accompanying code. It consists of a simple stack of convolutional layers, followed by batch normalization and pooling layers. Readers are encouraged to experiment with alternative architectures, as \textit{CNN}-based approaches for image quality assessment are known to be both efficient and accurate, even when implemented using relatively simple network designs.

Once the \textit{CNN} scorer is trained, it can be used to evaluate and select the most accurate segmentation result. This final decision-making step is carried out as described in Section~\ref{subsec:step6}.

\subsection{Optimal segmentation step}
\label{subsec:step6}

The final segmentation comprises of three stages.

\subsubsection{Rough stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage1}

The final segmentation algorithm builds upon the approach described in Section~\ref{subsec:step4}. We continue to perform the Rough Segmentation Step (\textit{RSS}), but in this phase, segmentation is carried out at scales ranging from 20\% to 100\%, with a step size of 20\%. From the five resulting segmentation candidates, the one deemed most accurate by the trained \textit{CNN} (introduced in Section~\ref{subsec:step5}) is selected as the final output. The scale at which this optimal segmentation occurs is referred to as the \textit{RSOR}.

It is important to note that for smaller images (up to 800$\times$800 pixels), we increase the maximum scale to 150\% in order to expand the range of considered resolutions. This adjustment accounts for the fact that \textit{U-Net} may underperform when operating on very low-resolution inputs.

\subsubsection{Detailed stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage2}

The \textit{RSOR} ratio plays a crucial role, as it serves as the starting point for the subsequent stage, known as the Detailed Segmentation Step (\textit{DSS}). This step further refines the search for the optimal segmentation scale. Specifically, segmentation is performed at scales ranging from $\textit{RSOR} - 10\%$ to $\textit{RSOR} + 10\%$, using a step size of 4\%. As before, the trained \textit{CNN} from Section~\ref{subsec:step5} is used to evaluate the quality of each segmentation. The scale identified as producing the best result is referred to as the \textit{DSOR}.

\subsubsection{Most detailed stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage3}

With the \textit{DSOR} ratio now established, we proceed to the Most Detailed Segmentation Step (\textit{MDSS}). In this final stage, the input image is rescaled according to the selected \textit{DSOR} value. Unlike in Section~\ref{subsec:step4}, where the window sliding step (\textit{SS}) was set to 64, we now adopt a finer step size of 32 to enhance segmentation precision.

The results of this final segmentation step, applied to a sample image from the \textit{DRHAGIS} dataset \cite{holm2017drhagis}, are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap2}. It is important to note that both the binarized segmentation outputs and the probability heatmaps, originally generated at the scaled resolution, are rescaled back to the original image dimensions for consistency and visualization.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[height=0.1\textheight]{figures/segment_rough_original.png} & \\
