It is worth noting that all configuration parameters associated with window size (\textit{WS}), sliding step (\textit{SS}), and the scale percentages used for segmentation are fully adjustable by users of the implementation provided in \cite{palles77unetsegmentation}. 

In Section~\ref{sec:experiments}, we present a series of experiments evaluating segmentation accuracy using the Jaccard Index across a combined dataset composed of three sources: \textit{DRHAGIS} \cite{holm2017drhagis}, \textit{HRF} \cite{budai2013robust}, and \textit{STARE} \cite{hoover2000locating}. This merged dataset is hereafter referred to as the Combined Validation Dataset (\textit{CVDS}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

In our experiments, we aimed to evaluate how well the proposed approach generalizes when a \textit{U-Net} model trained on one dataset is applied to a completely different dataset.

We conducted a total of three experiments:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item In the first experiment, both the \textit{U-Net} and \textit{CNN} models were trained on 100 randomly selected images from the \textit{FIVES} dataset \cite{jin2022fives}. The trained models were then tested on the Combined Validation Dataset (\textit{CVDS}), which consists of 150 images.  
  \item In the second experiment, 900 randomly selected images from the \textit{FIVES} dataset were used to train both the \textit{U-Net} and \textit{CNN} models. As in the first experiment, the evaluation was performed on the \textit{CVDS}.  
  \item In the third experiment, we used the same \textit{U-Net} model trained in the first experiment, but applied a naive Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}), as described in Section~\ref{subsec:step4}.
\end{enumerate}

The purpose of these experiments was to assess whether the full pipeline, outlined in Sections~\ref{subsec:step1} through~\ref{subsec:step6}, achieves superior segmentation performance compared to the traditional Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}). 

The central hypothesis is that identifying an optimal resolution, at which the \textit{U-Net} model performs best for each individual image, leads to more accurate segmentation results than applying the model directly, at a fixed resolution in a brute-force manner.

The \textit{U-Net} architecture used for segmentation is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:unet_network}. As illustrated, the structure is simplified compared to the original version proposed in \cite{ronneberger2015unet}. The accompanying \textit{CNN} (Figure~\ref{fig:cnn_network}) was designed to be relatively lightweight, yet capable of providing a meaningful assessment of segmentation quality through learned evaluation.

