64 & 32 & 16 & 4091 & 0.6153 & 0.2819 & 0.7668 \\
64 & 64 & 8 & 7938 & 0.6143 & 0.2823 & 0.7601 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:experiment1}
\end{table}

The common parameters selected for the segmentation process are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Minimum rough segmentation scale: 20\%,
  \item Maximum rough segmentation scale: 100\%,
  \item Rough segmentation scale step: 20\%,
  \item Rough segmentation range for optimal resolution: $\pm$10\%,
  \item Detailed segmentation range for optimal resolution: $\pm$4\%.
\end{itemize}

The meanings of the column headings used in the evaluation tables are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{RWSS}: Rough Segmentation Window Step,
  \item \textit{DWSS}: Detailed Segmentation Window Step,
  \item \textit{FOWSS}: Final Optimal Window Step for Segmentation,
  \item \textit{TTE}: Total Time Elapsed (in seconds),
  \item \textit{AJI}: Average Jaccard Index,
  \item \textit{MNJI}: Minimum Jaccard Index,
  \item \textit{MXJI}: Maximum Jaccard Index.
\end{itemize}

The highest achieved \textit{AJI} was 0.6153; however, this result came at the cost of a total time elapsed (\textit{TTE}) of 12,877 seconds. In contrast, the fastest configuration, with a \textit{TTE} of just 2,704 seconds, was obtained using the parameters \textit{RWSS} = 128 pixels, \textit{DWSS} = 64 pixels, and \textit{FOWSS} = 32 pixels. This setup yielded an \textit{AJI} of 0.6125, which is only marginally lower than the best result. As such, it represents a significantly more efficient trade-off between segmentation accuracy and computational performance.

