The results in this experiment are notably inferior to those presented in Section~\ref{subsec:experiment1}. The best outcome observed here corresponds to a segmentation step (\textit{SS}) with a total time elapsed of 24,227 seconds and an \textit{AJI} of 0.5639. While this approach demonstrates high execution speed for \textit{SS} values ranging between 32 and 128 pixels, the resulting \textit{AJI} scores remain unsatisfactory.

In contrast, the fastest configuration from Section~\ref{subsec:experiment1} achieved an \textit{AJI} of 0.6125, clearly demonstrating the superiority of the hybrid approach described in this article over the traditional \textit{SWA}-based method.

\section{Discussion}

The experiments conducted in this article reveal several notable observations regarding the proposed \textit{HUS} algorithm:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item When using only 100 training images from the \textit{FIVES} dataset \cite{jin2022fives}, we achieved a higher accuracy of 0.6153 compared to 0.5957 obtained with 900 training images. This suggests that in Section~\ref{subsec:experiment2}, the \textit{U-Net} and \textit{CNN} models may have become overly specialized to the training data, resulting in decreased generalization to the test set.  
  \item In Experiments~\ref{subsec:experiment1} and ~\ref{subsec:experiment2}, we demonstrated that the hybrid method outperforms the classic \textit{SWA} approach by nearly 0.05 in \textit{AJI}, while maintaining comparable execution speeds.  
  \item Our experiments with \textit{HUS} also indicate that high values of the sliding step (\textit{SS}) are not necessarily critical for achieving optimal performance. The results suggest a possible correlation between hierarchical window sizes. Specifically, for a window size of $2^n$, typically associated with the first convolutional layer of \textit{U-Net}, the following configuration yields strong results: $\textit{RWSS} = 2^n$, $\textit{DWSS} = 2^{n-1}$, and $\textit{FOWSS} = 2^{n-2}$.
\end{enumerate}

The \textit{HUS} algorithm is well-documented and publicly available for general use and extension to other segmentation tasks \cite{palles77unetsegmentation}. It effectively addresses several limitations commonly associated with traditional \textit{U-Net} segmentation, including:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The requirement for training and test datasets to have similar resolutions.
  \item The substantial memory consumption of \textit{U-Net} when processing high-resolution images, particularly in the absence of the Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}).
  \item The lack of a consistent correlation between increased computational time, due to smaller \textit{SS} values, and actual improvements in segmentation accuracy.
  \item The sensitivity of classical \textit{U-Net} to variations in the spatial location of the target object within the input image.
\end{enumerate}

Furthermore, our findings indicate that \textit{U-Net} architectures can be simplified from the original design presented in \cite{ronneberger2015unet}, while still achieving favorable outcomes. 

\section{Conclusion}

In summary, the \textit{U-Net} architecture, when enhanced by the proposed Hybrid \textit{U-Net} Segmentation (\textit{HUS}) methodology, demonstrates substantial improvements in robustness, scalability, and adaptability for image segmentation tasks. By addressing key limitations of traditional \textit{U-Net} models, such as sensitivity to input resolution, high memory requirements, and inefficiencies associated with fixed-scale segmentation, the \textit{HUS} framework offers a more flexible and accurate alternative, especially for challenging applications in medical imaging.

Our experiments show that adaptive scale selection, guided by a trained scoring \textit{CNN}, yields more precise segmentation outcomes than classical fixed-resolution approaches such as the Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}). Additionally, the evaluation across multiple datasets illustrates that \textit{HUS} generalizes well, even when the training and testing data differ significantly in resolution and source, highlighting its potential for real-world clinical applications.

Future work will focus on several promising directions. First, we aim to determine the minimal number of training images necessary to maintain high segmentation accuracy. We hypothesize that with improved sampling strategies and model refinement, it may be possible to significantly reduce the annotation burden. Second, further research is needed to explore the potential for minimizing or even eliminating the use of multiple offset meshes in \textit{SWA} during scale selection. Streamlining this component of the pipeline could lead to meaningful reductions in computation time and memory usage, making the system more practical for deployment in resource-constrained environments.

Moreover, extending the \textit{HUS} methodology to other types of medical imaging, such as MRI, CT, and ultrasound, presents a valuable opportunity to validate its versatility and promote its adoption across a wider range of clinical segmentation tasks. As the landscape of medical AI continues to evolve, approaches like \textit{HUS} that balance performance with computational efficiency will play a pivotal role in advancing early disease detection and long-term patient monitoring.

